    "narrative": "Our offensive engine today is anchored by **Lawrence Butler** (73.17) and **Taylor Ward** (71.19), who both benefit significantly from the favorable matchup against Colin Rea. While **Taylor Ward** faces a negative dynamic platoon adjustment, the park factors and Rea\u2019s susceptibility to power hitters provide a high ceiling. **Jeremiah Jackson** (66.61) and **Dylan Beavers** (68.40) are also strong plays in this same matchup; despite the slight drag on their scores due to the \"Elite Hold\" status of the opposing battery, the raw park and skill advantages remain too potent to fade. **Cal Raleigh** (65.29) remains our most reliable backstop option, buoyed by a strong matchup against Tyler Phillips, where his favorable SP skill projection compensates for the platoon-based regression.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision involved **Daulton Varsho** (53.03), who narrowly missed the cut for the final outfield slot. While his platoon edge against Logan Webb was notable, the optimizer prioritized the higher cumulative efficiency of **Gavin Sheets** and **JJ Bleday**. The primary risk today is the widespread \"Lineup Pending\" status across our roster; we have projected positions for **Yainer Diaz** and **Zack Gelof**, but any significant drop in their respective batting orders would necessitate an immediate re-evaluation of their projected R and RBI output. Furthermore, **Bryce Harper** (55.55) enters as a marginal start; while his xwOBA remains elite, his score is severely constrained by the elite SP_xERA of 3.33 assigned to Chase Burns. We are playing these starters for their floor, but we remain vigilant regarding the volatile late-afternoon weather patterns that could impact the slugging potential of the core hitters facing Rea.",
